This course is aimed at providing the learner with knowledge, skills and competences required to work as Refrigeration and Air-conditioning Maintenance Personnel. Through this training programme, the learner will be able to identify and understand the correct practices used in this industry. It is also aimed at raising a sense of Health & Safety, Environmental Awareness, Customer Service and Work Communication.
The learner will be able to: 

a)	Use the relevant technologies to handle fluorinated greenhouse gases safely; 
b)	Apply work practices that comply with legislation on RAC systems.
c)	Identify, discuss and report a fault within a RAC system;
d)	Advise on the potential leakage points of refrigeration, air-conditioning and heat pump equipment;
e)	Carry out visual and manual inspections of the whole system for standard leakage requirements on RAC systems containing fluorinated greenhouse gases, so as to comply with local and EU regulations;
f)	Use industry standard recording of data in equipment records;
g)	Weigh refrigerant to the specified decimal place using the correct type of scale;
h)	Dismantle and reassemble components to drain/flush oil out of system;
i)	Calculate the quantity of flushing agent required;
j)	Determine the type of contaminant within the system;
k)	Select the appropriate procedure and agent to flush the system;
l)	Decide when to declare a system clean of contaminants. The learner will be able to: 

a)	Understand the importance of maintaining and using tools and equipment in a manner which complies with health and safety requirements;
b)	Be familiar with handling procedures relating to hazardous materials; 
c)	Know about the use and safe handling of fluorinated greenhouse gases and other substances as refrigerants;
d)	Identify possible Fluorinated-gas replacement gases;
e)	Be familiar with legislation relevant to RAC systems, their installation, repair, maintenance and decommission;
a)	List the basic ISO standard units for temperature, pressure, mass density & energy;
b)	Name and describe the function of different components of a RAC system including but not limited to; Compressors, condensers and evaporators;
c)	List the different types of AC filters;
d)	List the copper pipe categories of RAC;
e)	Recall procedures to connect and disconnect the equipment with minimal emissions;
f)	Recall the important precautions required when taking readings from a scale in order to obtain accurate results; 
g)	List the type of Ozone friendly flushing agent.

In this module the learner will acquire more advanced competences to perform effectively within a group of performers.
At the end of the module the learner will have been exposed to: 

a) all key-signatures (5 sharps and 5 flats) and various irregular time-signatures (e.g.  5/4, 7/4, and mixture of time signatures in the same piece);
b) Advanced ornamentation (e.g. mordent, trill);
c) music from selected styles and periods;
d) unpredictable performance situations e.g. change of environmental parameters;
e) methods of adjusting intonation whilst performing (e.g. adjusting length of instrument - brass and wind instrumentalists);
f) public performance in a chamber group (including professional performances, soloist, chamber and orchestral);
g) various techniques on how to achieve different textures in tone quality (e.g. for piano - use of wrists, hands, arms);
h) being familiar with the context of the material being tackled; 
i) correct posture and a reliable technique; 
j) a variety of dynamics (e.g. grading crescendos/diminuendos over longer phrases) and other interpretative elements including phrasing and articulation;
k) relating  theoretical elements to practice. At the end of the module the learner will have acquired the responsibility and autonomy to:

a) perform a musical work with adequate expression and stylistic interpretation;
b) execute music with an adequate sense of tone, intonation, flexibility and projection;
c) perform from memory selected pieces of music;
d) perform other repertoire pieces of the same level;
e) perceive and relate to the audience when performing in front of others;
f) address issues related to stage confidence and presence. At the end of the module, the learner will be able to:

a) read and play notation relevant to this level;
b) follow musical markings as indicated in the score (including tempo, dynamics and articulation: molto allegro, larghetto and andantino, martellato;
c) demonstrate a good understanding of how to adjust issues related to tone and intonation during performance e.g. adjusting the tension of the bow;
d) demonstrate a good understanding of the score structure and take the initiative to experiment with different interpretations e.g.  different tempi;
e) prepare, present and faithfully render the music score with intensity keeping in mind the composer’s intentions; 
f) practise and perform with the help of technological aids;
g) be responsible for the preparation of the required study work in good time for each lesson and/or performance.  A record of the level of this prepared work will be reflected on the mark given on the journal;
h) follow a study routine based on warm-up, scales studies, technical studies and core repertoire material. The student will be asked to play the exercises during the lessons and will be marked accordingly in the journal.

This subject area introduces recruits to several crimes that present a challenge to law enforcement. Recruits will be invited to understand the dynamics of each crime and how they differ from one another.
Unit 10A:  General Intro into Contemporary Crime - Understand the complexities and challenges presented by different forms of crime; Differentiate between categories of offenders. Unit 10B:  Cybercrime and computer misuse - Report cybercrime and computer misuse occurrences in the most appropriate and the best possible manner through the legislative knowledge acquired through this course regarding the side of the world they are familiar with involving technology and its underpinnings; Understand the importance of the role played by the members of the Police Cyber Crime Unit and the results they get through the processing of digital evidence in criminal investigations. Unit 10C:  Online Child abuse - Identify child sexual abuse material and report this offensive online content in the appropriate manner, in order to protect the victims and take down the said content so that the victims would not be abused further; Contribute towards the safeguarding of children who live in the neighbourhood by assisting in the risk management of individual offenders. Unit 10D:  Human Trafficking - Approach a case of human trafficking comprehensively, with the appropriate awareness, enforcement and support measures for victims which such a complex crime deservedly merits; Interpret, engage with and process human trafficking crimes by implementing the policies and legislation that underpin trafficking in human beings and other issues relevant to the victims of this crime, learned through the material passed on to the recruits during this study unit. Unit 10E:  Hate Crime - Raise awareness, foster empathy and address attitude aspects in colleagues and subordinates, towards specific groups and communities; Provide practical instructions on handling the various stages of the procedure to follow regarding hate crimes, adapted to the role and functions of the police constable; Provide practical instructions on approaching, helping and supporting victims, adapted to the role and functions of the police constable. Unit 10F:  Drug Trafficking - Understand and implement law enforcement principles in connection with how to combat drug trafficking including searches and recognition of the various forms of drugs; Identify, acknowledge, and observe trends and developments in drug production and trafficking; international coordinated approaches and cross-border investigations against organized crime groups. Unit 10G:  Illegal Immigration - Recognize the different types of immigration documents presented at port controls and be aware of the subtle ways that fraudulent documents can be distinguished from genuine specimens; Understand the main transnational investigation techniques as
well as developments and initiatives taken at EU level to combat illegal immigration and to disrupt activities of Organized Crime Groups. Unit 10H:  Organised Crime - Understand the crimes that fall under the concept of Organized Crime which is a category of transnational, national or local groupings of highly centralized enterprises run by criminals to engage in illegal activity, most commonly for profit but can sometimes also be politically motivated such as in the case of terrorist groups. Unit 10I:  Terrorism and Radicalisation - Be familiar with national and international law enforcement mechanisms and collaboration against international terrorism; Be able to notice any radicalization signs in any person and take action to notify the Counter Terrorism Unit accordingly. 
Unit 10J:  Theft of Historical and Cultural Artefacts - Understand the different categories of items which are commonly stolen for profit which include: Antique artefacts; Books; Furniture; Coins; Weapons; Gold and silver ware. Be aware of the illicit excavation of archaeological and paleontological items which damages historical sites and reduces the opportunity to know more about them when an item is removed from the site or the walls cave in due to digging or removing props holding up the structure. Unit 10K: Property Crime - Understand the importance of: Recording and analyzing property crime statistics; The harrowing and long-lasting effects of property crime on victims; Keeping abreast of current techniques used by criminals to commit property crime; The immediately response to property crime complaints; Perimeter area searches; Careful entering and searching of premises where property crime has been committed; Thinking like the property criminal; Neighbourhood watch systems; Neighbourhood canvassing; 	Property criminal tactics and police reaction; Investigative procedures and property recovery techniques; Dissemination of property crime prevention techniques; Undercover operations to infiltrate property crime organizations. Unit 10L:  Corruption -
Recognise the importance of and implementing the knowledge acquired regarding the following: The difference between direct and indirect police corruption; The conditions that breed police corruption; The possible temptations faced by the police; The impact of police corruption on democracy and development; The proper way to bring to justice those who are corrupt through the support of enforcement personnel and institutions set up for the same such purpose. Unit 10M:  Economic Crime - Identify timely opportunities for financial investigations; Recognize crime patterns on various forms of money laundering which is cash-based, electronic, underground banking or virtual currency; Recognize the role of the competent authorities – FIUs, police, tax and customs agencies, the financial sector in the prevention/control mechanisms; Explore common approaches to investigate money laundering and organized financial crime; Evaluate evidence and intelligence in financial crime. Unit 10N:  Pick Pocketing - Identify the most frequent and common scenes where the ideal environment for pickpocketing occurs: Bags left unattended on the beach and the shore; Easily accessible pockets in backpacks and handbags worn by people in crowded places such as concerts, feasts and entertainment areas as well as on popular bus routes where the buses are packed with tourists and local passengers; Easily accessible money wallets and mobile phones in back pockets. Unit 10O:  Prostitution and Sexual Offences - Contribute towards investigations of illegal prostitution and sexual offences and know how to access and use data concerning all perpetrators and victims to help in such investigations; Understand that some sectors of prostitution have links with several social issues such as domestic abuse, drug dependency, poverty, homelessness and social exclusion; Recognize that a large section of sex workers chooses this type of work for personal reasons and conduct their lives in a very professional way and many never even come to the attention of police either as victims or perpetrators of crime. Unit 10P:  Environmental Crime - Understand the latest crime patterns of cross-border environmental crime including waste, wildlife trafficking and illegal logging; Recognize effective enforcement methods to address the cross-border angle of the threat and corruption that enables to  circumvent administrative controls. Unit 10Q:  Homicide and Violent Crime - Develop an awareness of the different types of homicide, violent crimes and the important part that forensic science plays in the investigations of both; Determine possible offender characteristics which can be deduced from the evidence left behind, such as behavioral and personality components, ‘modus operandi’, case linkages, offender signatures, crime scene staging and evidence or body disposal choices. Unit 10R:  Interpersonal Violence
a)	Be aware of violence prevention strategies existing in the local context; 
•	Understand that interpersonal violence can occur between: Family members and intimate partners;
•	Acquaintances;
•	Strangers (when the violence is not intended to further the aims of any formally defined group or cause); 
b)	Understand that interpersonal violence can occur is such places as:
•	The home;
•	The street;
•	Public places;
•	The workplace;
•	Schools;
•	Hospitals;
•	Prisons;
•	Residential care facilities for the elderly or disabled.

Unit 10S:  Illegal Gambling - Be fully aware of the situation in Malta regarding illegal gambling, including that of match fixing; Be proficient regarding the spotting of possible enclosed and out of the public eye locations that illegal gambling rings might choose to set up their secret premises; Become proficient at reading the mannerisms and behaviours of the few but important members of the group behind illegal gambling rings. Unit 10T:  Case Scenarios: Real Life Situation Simulations (Collaborating all sessions together and put into practice) - Recognize and work upon the feedback received regarding mistakes committed during the training received through case scenarios set for this same purpose; Apply the valuable knowledge gained during case scenario, real life situation experience training which is set in a safe environment and under supervision of the instructor. Unit 10A:  General Intro into Contemporary Crime
a)	The type of crimes reported and processed by the Malta police;
b)	The kind of law enforcement response to the different categories of crime.

Unit 10B:  Cybercrime and computer misuse
a)	The legislative computer terms and regulations found between Chapter 9, Art 337B and 337H to enhance the recruit’s ability to understand the legislation in connection with Cybercrime and computer misuse; 
b)	The evolving crime landscape given the ever-increased reliance on technology.

Unit 10C:  Online Child abuse
a)	An overview of child sexual abuse material including the different categories related to this content;
b)	How to recognize a situation of child online abuse and how to protect the child from further harm. 

 Unit 10D:  Human Trafficking
a)	The definition of human trafficking in terms of Article 485A – 485G of the Criminal Code, Cap 9 of the laws of Malta;
b)	The difference between the terms and crimes of ‘people smuggling’ and ‘human trafficking’;
c)	The elements that make up a human trafficking offence in terms of the international legal obligations applicable, since a case encountered may be required to be prosecuted in a court of law;
d)	The concept of human trafficking from a law enforcement perspective.

Unit 10E:  Hate Crime
Information and guidance regarding:
a)	The basic concepts of National, EU and International legal policy framework and its application in connection with hate crime;
b)	The legal instruments and standards, as well as their application, in connection with relevant case-law of national and supranational courts such as the European Court of Human Rights; 
c)	The institutional framework, as regards cooperation with other authorities or actors at local, national or European level. 
Unit 10F:  Drug Trafficking
a)	National legislation dealing with substance abuse in Malta (Medical and Kindred Professions Ordinance (Cap 31), Dangerous Drugs Ordinance (Cap 101) and the Drugs
Dependence (Treatment not Imprisonment Act 2014);
b)	Knowledge and experience through discussions and the exchange of good practice and information regarding the support given by EU agencies, drug related EMPACT trafficking priority areas and how to develop problem-solving strategies. 

Unit 10G:  Illegal Immigration
a)	The legal basis of national immigration law and how to deal with migrants;
b)	The EU policy framework to address irregular migration;
c)	Case studies of recent human smuggling investigations and the results thereof.

Unit 10H:  Organised Crime
a)	How criminal organizations force people to do business with them, such as when a gang extorts money from shopkeepers for protection;
b)	How to distinguish similarities and differences between corruption, bribery, extortion and money laundering.

Unit 10I:  Terrorism and Radicalisation
a)	The process of radicalization, the breeding ground, the signals and the vulnerabilities;
b)	The difference between terrorism and other major crimes;
c)	The concept of multiculturalism and ‘policing for all’;
d)	The importance of intelligence gathering.
Unit 10J:  Theft of Historical and Cultural Artefacts 
a)	The basics of Chapter 445 of the Laws of Malta (Cultural Heritage Act) and other related aspects in Chapter 9 (Criminal Code);
b)	The considerable increase in the destruction of cultural heritage due to armed conflict;
c)	The theft of underwater cultural heritage which includes monuments, shipwrecks, and artefacts, which have been under water for at least 100 years.

Unit 10K: Property Crime
a)	A clear definition of what is meant by the category ‘Property Crime’, which usually involves private property, and which among other things represents:
Burglary, larceny, theft, motor vehicle/bicycle theft, arson, shoplifting, embezzlement and vandalism;
b)	A clear definition of property crime which is committed to enrich the perpetrator who wants to obtain money, property or some other kind of benefit and may involve force, or the threat of force, in cases like robbery or extortion and can result in two
outcomes, destroyed or stolen property;
c)	The Maltese, EU and International legislation covering property crime. 

Unit 10L:  Corruption
a)	The definition of corruption and the reasons why it is of the utmost importance to prevent it;
b)	What the term ‘corruption’ constitutes in connection with the daily duties of the police constable and a list of the most common types of corruption usually committed by police officers;
c)	The anti-corruption laws and their consequences on police officers;
d)	The national anti-fraud and corruption strategies and measures published by the Force to prevent, detect, punish and eradicate
corruption in the society.

Unit 10M:  Economic Crime
a)	The key types of financial crime including fraud, money laundering, terrorist financing, bribery and corruption;
b)	The impact of underground banking, the use of virtual currencies and other emerging technologies on financial investigations;
c)	The prevalent legislation regulating financial crime and money laundering including its impact on the financial services sector and its impact on the economy as a whole;
d)	The national and international structures in place for financial investigation assistance;
e)	How to assess good practices on forensic financial analysis, risk analysis and profiling;

Unit 10N:  Pick Pocketing
a)	The way that organized pickpocketing gangs operate and the difference between mugging and pickpocketing;
b)	The reasons behind the fact that pickpocketing gangs are usually connected to other organized crimes;
c)	The strategy which the Department of Criminal Investigations has put together to fight against petty theft and curb waves of pickpocketing as they occur.

Unit 10O:  Prostitution and Sexual Offences 
a)	The prevalent provisions in the Criminal code and the White Slave Traffic Ordinance in respect of prostitution and sexual offences;
b)	The information that although prostitution in itself is not illegal in Malta, it is however an offence for a person of any gender, to persistently loiter or solicit in a street or public place in order to offer their services as a prostitute, get paid for sexual services, operate or own a brothel, advertise prostitution or engage in kerb crawling, where a person solicits another in a street or public place for the purposes of prostitution;
c)	The range of crimes that can be considered as sexual offences such as:
•	Non-consensual crimes like rape, sexual assault or sexual touching, stalking, sexual harassment;
•	Crimes against children including child sexual abuse or grooming;
•	Crimes that exploit others for a sexual purpose, whether in person or online;
•	Crimes known as ‘honour-based’ violence including forced marriage, female genital mutilation, human trafficking focusing on sexual exploitation, prostitution, pornography, and obscenity.

Unit 10P:  Environmental Crime
a)	The principles of national legislation in respect of Environmental Crime including how to preserve and tender evidence in court;
b)	How to act against illegal activities involving Environmental Crime such as littering, dumping, polluting, participating in illegal trade of endangered species/fauna and hunting.

Unit 10Q:  Homicide and Violent Crime
a)	The definition of homicide and violent crimes and what is considered as such a crime;
b)	The physical evidence found most often at a homicide or violent crime scene and the proper way in which to collect, preserve and present it as evidence in court.

Unit 10R:  Interpersonal Violence
a)	The definition of and what constitutes interpersonal violence, family or partner violence and community violence;
b)	The basic national, EU and international laws which cover interpersonal violence;
c)	The pervasive challenge represented by interpersonal violence on public health, fundamental rights, equality, human capital, national and local economies and development in general;
d)	How interpersonal violence effects reverberate through families, communities, nations and across generations;
e)	How it is a leading cause of death among adolescents and young adults in most parts of the world;
f)	How exposure to interpersonal violence increases individuals’ lifelong vulnerability to a broad range of emotional, behavioural and physical health problems.

Unit 10S:  Illegal Gambling
a)	The laws that make gambling illegal in Malta: 
•	Criminal Code Chapter 9, Contraventions affecting Public Order, 338, ii; 
•	Gaming Act, Laws of Malta, Cap 583;
•	The Lotteries and other Games Act, Laws of Malta, Cap 438;
•	The Racecourse Betting Ordinance, Cap 78.
b)	The typical model set-up of an illegal gambling ring comprised of:
•	The head bookmaker, usually the top member of the ring;
•	The operations/site manager who ensures the safe and smooth operation of the ring;
•	The dealers;
•	The tellers;
•	The bet processors;
•	The telephone operators;
•	The debt collectors;
c)	The proper way in which to report any suspicious activity through the proper channels.

Unit 10T:  Case Scenarios: Real Life Situation Simulations (Collaborating all sessions together and put into practice)
a)	The things that might and will go wrong during the performing of a police constables’ daily work schedule;
b)	How to react calmly and stay in control of any given situation during the line of duty, having faced similar situations during case scenario training in a ‘learning by error’ environment. Unit 10A:  General Intro into Contemporary Crime
a)	Analyze and categorize crime according to the nature of the offence and the modus operandi preferred by the offender;
b)	Compare and contrast the different modus operandi used by criminals to exploit victims in several crimes.

Unit 10B:  Cybercrime and computer misuse
a)	Understand the work covered by and the responsibilities of the Police Cyber Crime Unit and its provision of technical assistance in the detection and investigations of crime wherein the computer is the target, or the means used;
b)	Employ the necessary toolbox of skills taken out of this course to effectively take valid appropriate reports and investigate a crime involving a technology device.

Unit 10C:  Online Child abuse
a)	Understand the importance of a holistic, whole family approach to the investigation, the related police and multi-agency action and the connections and coordination necessary for the benefit of the victims;
b)	Work on and increase own listening and empathy skills.

Unit 10D:  Human Trafficking
a)	Identify elements that make human trafficking a crime and become sensitized about the situation and the fundamental rights of the victims encountered;
b)	Employ the learned skills of thorough observation, identification of the perpetrators/victims and recognition of 
indicator signs acquired through this unit to better serve the core functions of a police constable in relation to this complex crime.

Unit 10E:  Hate Crime
Transfer and develop practical skills to deal with hate crimes more effectively and ensure adequate support to victims through:
a)	Gathering and investigating evidence;
b)	Collecting, categorizing and processing data; 
c)	Carrying out interviews with victims and perpetrators;
d)	Identifying and recording of hate crimes based on bias indicators;
e)	Needs assessments;
f)	Referrals to support services;
g)	The application of the relevant legislation in the prosecution and sentencing phase;
h)	Protecting the citizen’s right to information.
Unit 10F:  Drug Trafficking
a)	Participate in discussions to help increase the possibility of fighting the production and trafficking of different types of synthetic drugs, including new psychoactive substances;
b)	Acknowledge and increase own interest in the topic by following more in-depth study on a national and international level. 
Unit 10G:  Illegal Immigration
a)	Understand the importance of the role of Immigration officers and the Refugee Commissioner;
b)	Become familiar with the latest trends on routes and ‘modus operandi’ of human smugglers.

Unit 10H:  Organised Crime
a)	Identify and distinguish the impact and the relationship that organized crime has on crimes involving trafficking, criminal gangs, narcotics and terrorism;
b)	Understand how to determine what kind of level of organization a criminal gang has reached and whether it is a mob, ring, mafia, syndicate, network, subculture or part of the underworld.  

Unit 10I:  Terrorism and Radicalisation
a)	Demonstrate familiarity with the origins and uses of terrorism and its re-emergence at different times in different locales;
b)	Be aware of ways and means to safeguard and support those most at risk of radicalization through early identification and
intervention;
c)	Be familiar with the support units which can help those who have already engaged in terrorism and need to disengage and rehabilitate.
Unit 10J:  Theft of Historical and Cultural Artefacts 
a)	Be aware of the steps to take in case of historical and cultural crime cases and what to look out for;
b)	Understand that crimes committed against historical and cultural heritage do not just strike at the objects stolen or destroyed themselves but are directly linked to the persecution of individuals and communities on cultural grounds and may also represent a security and stability issue or a war crime;
c)	Apply knowledge to identify and distinguish theft of art works from private homes, museums and places of worship which include paintings, sculptures, statues and religious items, all very sought after by thieves.

Unit 10K: Property Crime
a)	Recognize and distinguish when and how a property crime has been committed by forcible entry, unlawful entry without the use of force and attempted forcible entry;
b)	Become familiar with the underlying causes of property crime such as poverty, substance abuse/drug addiction and peer group pressure;
c)	Give advice to citizens on ways to decrease the chance of becoming victims of property crime, such as installing effective security systems and alarms in the home, commercial establishments, and vehicles. 

 Unit 10L:  Corruption
a)	Respond ethically and professionally to bribes, corruption and abuse of power and position;
b)	Understand and facilitate measures to prevent corruption by:
•	Targeting and analyzing information about the main facilitators and enablers of corruption; 
•	Developing transparency standards and creating relevant legislation in the fight against corruption; 
c)	Utilize effective risk assessment techniques and mechanisms such as all laws, policies, codes of conduct, and oversight bodies to monitor the systems and measures established for preventing, detecting, punishing, and eradicating police corruption.
Unit 10M:  Economic Crime
a)	Understand the role and relevant procedures of various international actors, such as Europol, Eurojust and OLAF and networks such as FIU, CARIN, the Egmont Group and ALEFA;
b)	Understand the characteristics of financial intelligence related to terrorism financing, the different forms of serious organized 
crime, economic crime or tax fraud; 
c)	Apply specific methods and techniques of managing, analyzing 
and evaluating financial data;
d)	Recognize the various types of predicative offences, such as different forms of serious organized crime, economic crimes or tax fraud;
e)	Become aware of techniques which help with asset identification, tracing, recovery, valuation and management measures.
Unit 10N:  Pick Pocketing
a)	Understand the way that undercover police operate in popular areas when the number of pickpocketing occurrences increase;
b)	Identify the items which are the easiest for pickpockets to lift, including wallets, purses, money, credit cards, mobiles, watches, jewellery and expensive sunglasses;
c)	Advise citizens about how to take good care while carrying their valuables and money and how to avoid falling victim to pickpocketing gangs.

Unit 10O:  Prostitution and Sexual Offences
a)	Have an awareness of the functioning of the sex industry;
b)	Have an awareness of causing, inciting or controlling prostitution for gain and trafficking in human beings for sexual exploitation;	
c)	Identify and cooperate with the appropriate leading units covering crimes of prostitution and sexual offences, coordinate and assist in investigations to the best possible level and ensure attendance in strategic multi-agency forums on both types of crime.

Unit 10P:  Environmental Crime
a)	Understand some important principles of the CITES Convention (The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora);
b)	Understand the importance behind the application of financial investigations in environmental crime cases.

Unit 10Q:  Homicide and Violent Crime
a)	Gain an understanding of investigative strategies used in the detection of serious violent offenders;
b)	Demonstrate an understanding of the criminal trial process and the importance tied to items which can be produced as evidence in court, such items including:
Blood and other body fluids;
Weapons and tools;
Fingerprints, impressions and marks;
Trace evidence like hair, fibre, glass, paint and soil;
Documents, computer, and forensic toxicological evidence. 

Unit 10R:  Interpersonal Violence
a)	Demonstrate an understanding of the roles of NGOs in the work concerning cases of 
interpersonal violence;
b)	Understand that family or partner violence refers to violence against members of the perpetrator’s family or intimate partners and that it also includes the maltreatment of children or the elderly and violence on dating partners;
c)	Understand that community violence means intentional acts of interpersonal violence committed in public areas by individuals who are not intimately related to the victim but may be known to them and includes youth violence, bullying, assault, rape or sexual assault by acquaintances or strangers.

Unit 10S:  Illegal Gambling
a)	Become familiar and understand the reasoning behind the most recent strategies developed in the fight against illegal gambling in Malta;
b)	Understand the gravity and risk underlying these unregulated ‘dark’ activities which implement extortion and violence to run their business and are not required to assist victims who get addicted to gambling;
c)	Empathize with gambling addicts who face ruin when illegal gambling rings withhold pay-outs or fix impossible odds on matches which create more substantial losses to the individual.

 Unit 10T:  Case Scenarios: Real Life Situation Simulations (Collaborating all sessions together and put into practice) 
a)	Control own emotions and remain impartial while:
•	Controlling situations;
•	Securing a crime scene;
•	Assisting victims;
•	Processing suspects;
•	Gathering of evidence;
b)	Reach higher levels of subject-specific, police-oriented, thinking skills for decision-making, problem solving and collaboration.

In order to maintain quality and safe care, one must be aware of how to protect and prevent themselves and other stakeholders from any infections and infectious diseases. 
This module will introduce students to basics of infection and prevention control knowledge and skills which are carried out routinely within all clinical settings. 

This module focuses on the Maltese legislations related to the sector.
•	Develop a sound body of theoretical knowledge underpinning a number of practice skills involved in infection prevention and control;
•	Follow policies, procedures, and tools regarding infection prevention and control;
•	Develop a basic awareness of the legal aspect in infection control practices;
•	Share information about infection control and prevention as relevant to all care settings and of the services offered in public health on infection control;
•	Adhere to outbreak management plans in case of a medical emergency. •	Explain the infection control and prevention process according to standard procedures;
•	Describe the procedure to maintain hand hygiene and disinfected envelopment to avoid and prevent infection;
•	*Provide and maintain a clean and disinfected caring environment free from hazards;
•	*Use of personal protective clothing and equipment (PPE) as applicable;
•	*Avoid needle stick injuries and how they can be avoided.

*Skill presentation is recommended to be endorsed during placement •	Defining infectious diseases 
•	Types of infections e.g., viral, bacterial, fungal, others
•	The Disease transmission Cycle, and modes of infection transmission;
•	Principles of epidemiology to environmental risk assessment;
•	The Development of a comprehensive infection prevention and control program;
•	Infection control data samples;
•	Management protocols of any outbreak.
•	Basic legal aspects
•	Public services
•	Policies and procedures re infection prevention and control
